    "description": "The Asiatic cheetah is a Critically Endangered cheetah subspecies whose confirmed surviving wild population is confined to Iran. Its conservation depends on protecting extensive dryland habitats, maintaining wild prey, and distinguishing documented animals from uncertain population estimates.",
    "tags": ["Asiatic cheetah", "Wildlife conservation", "Dryland habitats", "Iranian biodiversity", "Conservation monitoring"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T12:24:51Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T12:24:51Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Asiatic Cheetah: Iran’s Surviving Wild Population | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand the Asiatic cheetah’s Iranian habitat, conservation history, and the limits of population estimates for this Critically Endangered subspecies."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "identity-and-habitat",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A cheetah of Iran’s drylands",
        "content": "The Asiatic cheetah, *Acinonyx jubatus venaticus*, is a subspecies of the cheetah rather than a separate species. The IUCN SSC Cat Specialist Group, a network of specialists supporting conservation assessment and planning, identifies Iran as its remaining confirmed wild stronghold and describes the subspecies as Critically Endangered. This assessment concerns the Asiatic subspecies; it should not be confused with the conservation category assigned to cheetahs worldwide. [s1]\n\nA cheetah has solid dark spots and conspicuous dark lines running down its face. A leopard’s markings form rosettes, making the two cats distinguishable even though both occur in Iran. Iranian cheetah habitat includes semi-desert, dry open country, eroded foothills, and mountainous desert terrain. The landscape is therefore more varied than an uninterrupted expanse of sand. Cover for hunting and resting remains important within these relatively open environments. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "population-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why population figures require context",
        "content": "Published cheetah numbers need their original dates and definitions. A conservation news article published in Oryx in August 2022 gave an estimate of 12 free-ranging animals while explicitly excluding particular cubs and potentially additional animals without independently identifiable photographs. That was a qualified account at a particular moment, not a complete census that can be carried forward as Iran’s population in 2026. [s2]\n\nThe same article documented a captive birth in May 2022 and subsequent cub deaths, raising concerns about husbandry and specialist experience. Captive births, surviving captive animals, and free-ranging animals answer different conservation questions. Combining them into a single headline total would conceal those differences. The authors advocated cooperation between Iranian authorities, nongovernmental organizations, and experienced international specialists. Their recommendations establish what they considered necessary; they do not demonstrate that recovery subsequently occurred. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "conservation-in-practice",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Protection involves people as well as habitat",
        "content": "The United Nations Development Programme’s account of the Conservation of Asiatic Cheetah Project describes protected-area management, support for game guards, community awareness, and negotiations over grazing and water rights. Iran’s Department of Environment is the government institution responsible for the project’s environmental implementation. The account reports an agreement with herders to move grazing away from a core area in Touran National Park through an incentive package. These measures illustrate why a reserve boundary alone cannot settle competing uses of dryland resources. [s3]\n\nUNDP also describes investigating cheetah deaths to identify preventive measures. Such work links protection to evidence about actual causes of mortality. Its project page contains historical activities and an undated population statement, however, so neither should be treated as a verified account of field conditions at the editorial cutoff. [s3]\n\nThe practical conservation challenge is to sustain habitat, prey, and protection together. Historical range contraction has involved prey depletion, habitat alteration, and direct killing. [s1] Progress should consequently be judged through documented survival and reproduction alongside effective habitat protection, rather than through announcements or an isolated photograph alone. This is an editorial synthesis of the different kinds of evidence described above. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
