    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "The Persian leopard is a threatened leopard subspecies distributed across Southwest Asia, with Iran holding its largest population and important continuous habitat. Its conservation connects mountain ecology with livestock management, prey protection, and movement across fragmented landscapes.",
    "tags": ["Persian leopard", "Large carnivores", "Mountain habitats", "Human-wildlife conflict", "Conservation research"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T12:24:51Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T12:24:51Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Persian Leopard: Habitat and Conservation in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore the Persian leopard’s Iranian stronghold, mountain habitats, livestock conflicts, and research on detecting otherwise hidden mortality."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "identity-and-range",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A regional predator with an Iranian stronghold",
        "content": "The Persian leopard is a leopard subspecies distributed across Southwest Asia, rather than an animal confined to Iran. The IUCN SSC Cat Specialist Group uses the scientific name *Panthera pardus tulliana*; older Iranian studies often use *Panthera pardus saxicolor*. Readers comparing sources should recognize this naming difference. The specialist account classifies the Persian leopard as Endangered and identifies Iran as holding its largest population and extensive continuous suitable habitat. [s1]\n\nIranian records are especially associated with the Alborz Mountains and northeastern protected areas. Distribution and density are uneven, however, and a stronghold in one landscape cannot establish abundance elsewhere. The leopard uses forests, woodland, and scrub in mountainous terrain, with rocky outcrops and steep slopes forming important habitat features. It is also a wide-ranging animal whose movements extend beyond individual reserves. [s1]\n\nThese characteristics make the surrounding landscape consequential. Habitat fragmentation and border fencing can obstruct connections even where suitable habitat remains on either side. Maintaining a regional population therefore requires attention to movement as well as conditions inside protected areas. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "prey-and-livestock",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Understanding livestock predation",
        "content": "Leopard conservation intersects with livestock husbandry, but the presence of livestock in a predator’s diet does not by itself establish preference. Preference requires comparing consumption with the prey actually available. A study of Persian leopards in Golestan National Park explicitly addressed this methodological problem by combining analysis of droppings with assessments of wild and domestic prey abundance. It also incorporated information about when livestock grazed outside villages. [s2]\n\nThat approach matters because animals housed or closely guarded for part of the day are not continuously exposed to predators. Counting every domestic animal as equally available can distort comparisons with wild prey. The inspected abstract establishes the study’s design; detailed dietary results are not reproduced here because the full paper was not successfully inspected. [s2]\n\nThe broader lesson is interpretive: reports of livestock loss should be assessed alongside exposure, husbandry, and the local prey community. Evidence from a particular park should not become a universal description of leopard feeding throughout Iran. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "hidden-mortality",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Recorded deaths are an incomplete measure",
        "content": "A nationwide study published in the Journal of Applied Ecology in 2022 analyzed ranger-collected leopard killing records from 2007–2019. Rather than assuming that all deaths had been found, its model considered the probability that an event would be detected. Detection was higher in areas with more developed road networks. An apparent geographic concentration of records can therefore reflect both actual mortality and differences in the chances of discovering it. [s3]\n\nThe study associated expected killing intensity with livestock density, wild prey abundance, and protected-area size. These associations do not mean that wild prey or reserves inherently cause leopard deaths. They identify patterns requiring interpretation within landscapes where predators, prey, livestock, and observers overlap. The authors recommended improved grazing management and conflict mitigation around high-risk protected areas. [s3]\n\nFor readers assessing conservation progress, the implication is straightforward: a fall in recorded deaths is encouraging only when monitoring effort and detection are understood. Protecting prey, reducing livestock conflict, and sustaining movement between habitats require evidence that tracks both living animals and the losses that might otherwise remain unseen. This is an editorial synthesis of the monitoring and habitat evidence. [s1][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      }
    ],
